WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
The first film did the arithmetic.

2
00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:05.540
Spend two ATP, earn four, keep two.

3
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:10.100
But each half of the sugar carries one ordinary phosphate.

4
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.800
So how does each half hand two phosphates to ADP?

5
00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:21.213
Phosphates sit on a ladder, ranked by how eager they are to leave.

6
00:00:22.513 --> 00:00:25.913
ATP sits at −30.5 kJ/mol.

7
00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:32.633
On this standard ladder, a kinase can load ADP favorably only from a higher rung.

8
00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:36.973
The sugar half's phosphate is an ordinary one, far below.

9
00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:40.243
So the cell builds a higher rung.

10
00:00:41.203 --> 00:00:47.163
At step 6, an enzyme oxidizes the sugar half, and NAD+ plus takes the electrons.

11
00:00:48.643 --> 00:00:51.423
Done in one go, that energy would be lost as heat.

12
00:00:52.763 --> 00:00:55.803
Instead, the enzyme holds it in an activated bond.

13
00:00:56.883 --> 00:01:02.883
Then inorganic phosphate, from the cell's pool, not from ATP, attacks that bond and

14
00:01:02.883 --> 00:01:04.323
carries the sugar half away.

15
00:01:05.683 --> 00:01:09.843
Its new phosphate sits at −49.3 kJ/mol, well above ATP.

16
00:01:11.283 --> 00:01:13.883
At step 7, a kinase hands it to ADP.

17
00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:16.887
Here is the idea to hold.

18
00:01:18.147 --> 00:01:22.727
From glucose, the net gain is exactly the new phosphate that this oxidation

19
00:01:22.727 --> 00:01:28.287
captures. Everything else the cell hands to ADP is only paid back.

20
00:01:29.317 --> 00:01:33.357
But that old phosphate is still on the sugar half, below ATP.

21
00:01:34.617 --> 00:01:36.197
There is no second oxidation.

22
00:01:37.577 --> 00:01:39.017
So how can it make ATP?

23
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.480
Rearrange the molecule.

24
00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:51.280
Step 8 slides the phosphate from carbon 3 to carbon 2.

25
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.060
Step 9 pulls off water, making PEP.

26
00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.260
That barely changes the free energy.

27
00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:04.460
Yet PEP's phosphate sits at the top of the ladder, −61.9 kJ/mol.

28
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.440
The secret is what stays behind.

29
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.800
Once the phosphate leaves, the rest is an unstable form that quickly settles into

30
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.460
pyruvate. So this phosphate leaves very readily.

31
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.280
At step 10, a kinase hands it to ADP.

32
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.090
Now count phosphate groups for one glucose.

33
00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:27.530
Four leave to ADP.

34
00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:32.430
Two only repay the ATP spent at steps 1 and 3.

35
00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:37.110
Two are new, taken from inorganic phosphate at step 6.

36
00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.130
The oxidation paid for those two.

37
00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:44.633
So the two phosphates lent by ATP only break even.

38
00:02:45.933 --> 00:02:51.353
Every ATP glycolysis nets from glucose was paid for by the oxidation at step 6,

39
00:02:51.853 --> 00:02:53.853
which needs NAD+.
